{"authored" [{"question" "q-102808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-3=y \\\\ 3y=4x+2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041555/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a35cce6ef5b960efb55fbeefff0165f9791a554d68db697e65fd87a63ed6dfe" "basis" 1503 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041555/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=3 \\\\ -4x+3y=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041555/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a35cce6ef5b960efb55fbeefff0165f9791a554d68db697e65fd87a63ed6dfe" "basis" 1503 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041555/ed